 types of a Bunsen burner depend on oxygen supply. On the left a rich fuel with no premixed oxygen produces a yellow sooty diffusion flame; on the right a lean fully oxygen premixed flame produces no soot and the flame color is produced by molecular radicals, especially CH and C2 band emission.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABunsen_burner_flame_types.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/500px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/1280px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Flametest--Na.swn.jpg/500px-Flametest--Na.swn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A flame test for sodium. The yellow color in this gas flame does not arise from the black-body emission of soot particles (as the flame is clearly a blue premixed complete combustion flame) but instead comes from the spectral line emission of sodium atoms, specifically the very intense sodium D lines.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlametest--Na.swn.jpg"}],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"Woman's shirt from Kutch, Gujarat, India, IMA 55114","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Woman%27s_shirt_from_Kutch%2C_Gujarat%2C_India%2C_IMA_55114.jpg/500px-Woman%27s_shirt_from_Kutch%2C_Gujarat%2C_India%2C_IMA_55114.jpg","ratio":0.9394273127753304,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Woman%27s_shirt_from_Kutch,_Gujarat,_India,_IMA_55114.jpg","share":0.228125},{"title":"Veiled in Red","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Veiled_in_Red.jpg/500px-Veiled_in_Red.jpg","ratio":0.6772247360482655,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Veiled_in_Red.jpg","share":0.118125},